# build output
/magma
/dist/
*.out
cover.out

# scratch
/tmp/

# rust helper build output
/rust-helper/target/
# Every fixture's own target dir, by pattern rather than by a hand-maintained
# list of one line per fixture. The list version failed open exactly as you would
# expect: adding testdata/try_from and testdata/drop_glue without also adding two
# more lines here tracked 31 files of build output. `*` does not match `/`, so
# this still matches only the one directory level fixtures live at.
/rust-helper/testdata/*/target/

# Fixture lockfiles. Every testdata crate is dependency-free, so its lockfile
# pins nothing and is regenerated by each harness run — committing them would
# be pure churn. Real dependency lockfiles are not covered by this pattern.
/rust-helper/testdata/*/Cargo.lock

# Session working documents — handovers, plans, specs, research. Keep them on
# disk; keep them out of the repository. They are working state, not product,
# and the failure mode is specific rather than theoretical: a status document
# committed at the repo root reads as CURRENT to whoever opens it next, and goes
# stale silently because nothing gates it. HANDOFF.md described v0.1.0 as
# unfinished, and never mentioned Rust, well after v0.2.0 shipped with the Rust
# backend — it was the first thing a fresh session read, and it sent that session
# the wrong way.
#
# Durable reasoning does not go here. It goes where it cannot drift from the
# thing it describes: code comments for invariants (the Go backend's five
# correctness facts already live in golang.go and graph.go, with fuller rationale
# than the handover carried), and the README for anything user-facing.
/HANDOFF.md
/docs/superpowers/
/.superpowers/
